'''[[Okaiken]]''' is divided into 48 first-level administrative divisions known as {{nihongo|'''prefectures'''|都県|token}}. Ranking immediately below the federal government, these sectors form the country's primary level of jurisdiction. They consist of 46 standard {{nihongo|prefectures|県|ken}} and two {{nihongo|metropolises|都|to}}. 

Each prefecture is governed by a directly elected governor and a unicameral legislative assembly. Under the 1970 Constitution of the Federal Republic, prefectures hold significant localized power, retaining the authority to levy independent taxes, manage public education, organize prefectural police forces, and oversee regional infrastructure. 

==History and the Han Transition==
Unlike the sudden and forceful abolition of the {{nihongo|feudal domain system|廃藩置県|''haihan-chiken''}} seen in [[Nakamizu]] during the late 19th century, the establishment of Okaiken's prefectures was a slow, deliberate evolutionary process. 

For centuries under the [[Honda Shogunate]], the Sanese archipelago was divided into deeply entrenched ''han'' (feudal domains), each ruled by a powerful daimyo who answered to the Shogun in [[Okami]]. During the [[Saito Restoration]] of 1871, the Imperial faction realized that abruptly dismantling these domains would ignite a devastating, prolonged civil war. Instead, Emperor Saito and his western advisors implemented a transitional framework heavily influenced by the political structures of [[Volisania]] or [[Breisland]].

The original ''han'' borders were largely frozen in place, preserving the historical territorial lines of the clans. The ruling daimyo were not stripped of their lands; rather, they were officially re-titled as "Prefectural Governors" (知事, chiji) and integrated into a new, Illypnian-style bureaucratic hierarchy. Over the next several decades, these hereditary positions were gradually reformed into appointed bureaucratic roles, and eventually, into democratically elected offices. 

Because the modern prefectures are direct, unbroken continuations of the ancient ''han'', regional identity and historical clan loyalties remain deeply ingrained in the modern political and cultural fabric of Okaiken. 

==Types of prefectures==
Despite the different suffixes used in their Sanese names, there is no functional or legal difference in the autonomous power granted to the different types of prefectures under the federal government.

===To (Metropolis)===
Two of the 48 divisions are designated as a {{nihongo|metropolis|都|to}}: [[Okami Metropolis|Okami]] and [[Toyozawa Metropolis|Toyozawa]]. The character implies a core urban zone of absolute national or historical importance. Administratively, a metropolis functions exactly like a standard prefecture, but it has the unique authority to administer both standard cities and densely populated [[Special wards of Okami|special wards]] within its urban core. 

===Ken (Prefecture)===
The remaining 46 divisions are referred to as standard {{nihongo|prefectures|県|ken}}. The character historically carries a rural or provincial connotation, though several of Okaiken's ''ken'' (such as [[Miyagi Prefecture|Miyagi]] and [[Aizawa Prefecture|Aizawa]]) contain massive, highly industrialized metropolitan populations. 

